# Advisories pip-audit may pass over, with the reason and the way out.
#
# This file is the whole exception list. It applies ONLY to the dev lockfile
# scan - requirements.txt, which is what the image installs and what ships,
# is audited with no exceptions at all and blocks the release when it finds
# anything. That is the gate; this is the tooling.
#
# An entry here is a thing somebody decided, in a diff somebody reviewed. An
# entry with no reason, or whose reason has expired, is a bug. Review this
# file whenever `make lock` moves a pin.
#
# Format: one advisory id per line. `#` starts a comment.


# --- filelock 4.1.0, reviewed 2026-11-16 ----------------------------------
#
# Seventeen advisories, all the same shape: local symlink and TOCTOU attacks
# on the lock files that filelock itself creates - a planted symlink at the
# lock path, a stale-marker break that re-links a name it did not re-check,
# SQLite side files derived from an attacker-chosen directory.
#
# Three reasons they are passed over rather than fixed:
#
# 1. There is no fix to take. Every one of them names 4.2.1 as the fix
#    version and 4.2.1 is not published - 4.1.0 is still the newest release
#    on PyPI. Pinning forward is not an option that exists yet.
# 2. It does not ship. filelock arrives through pytest, through cachecontrol
#    under pip-audit, and through virtualenv under pre-commit. The Dockerfile
#    installs requirements.txt only, so none of it enters the image.
# 3. The preconditions are not met where it does run. Every advisory needs an
#    attacker who can already write to the directory holding the lock - a
#    developer's own cache directory, or a CI runner's workspace that is
#    created fresh for the job and destroyed with it.
#
# Out: when 4.2.1 reaches PyPI, `make lock` and delete this whole block. The
# scan fails the moment a filelock advisory appears that is not listed here,
# which is the point of listing them one by one rather than skipping the
# package.
